menu "Slate display"

config SLATE_BACKLIGHT_PWM
    bool "Backlight dimming through a GPIO-bridged PWM (hardware modification)"
    default n
    help
        Leave this off for a board as it ships. The CH422G drives the backlight
        enable as a plain output there, so a brightness percentage can only be
        acted on at 0 — which is what docs/CONFIGURATION.md documents.

        Turn it on only after bridging the backlight testpad to a free GPIO, as
        described in docs/backlight-dimming.md. The expander output stays the
        enable in both builds; this option adds an LEDC channel that carries the
        level. On an unmodified board a firmware built with it behaves exactly
        like one built without it, because the pin drives nothing.

config SLATE_BACKLIGHT_PWM_GPIO
    int "GPIO the backlight testpad is bridged to"
    depends on SLATE_BACKLIGHT_PWM
    default 16
    range 0 48
    help
        The installer chooses this pin, so there is no default that is right for
        everyone; 16 is the one the recipe in docs/backlight-dimming.md reaches
        through the RS485 jack.

        The pins this board has already spent are refused at bring-up with a log
        line, and the firmware falls back to on/off rather than fighting them:
        the sixteen RGB data lines, HSYNC, VSYNC, DE, PCLK, the I2C pair, the
        touch interrupt, GPIO26-37, which is where the module reaches its own
        flash and the PSRAM both framebuffers live in, and GPIO19/20, the USB
        Serial/JTAG pads an analog PHY holds in every image regardless of what
        the GPIO matrix is told.

        GPIO43/44 is accepted with a warning instead. UART0 is taken cleanly, so
        it does work — it just leaves the panel with no console to say so on.

config SLATE_BACKLIGHT_PWM_FREQ_HZ
    int "Backlight PWM frequency (Hz)"
    depends on SLATE_BACKLIGHT_PWM
    default 1000
    range 200 39000
    help
        1 kHz is what the ESPHome package for this board uses, which makes it
        the only frequency known to have driven this backlight. Raise it if the
        board whines audibly; the 10-bit duty resolution the firmware uses keeps
        every value in this range achievable.

config SLATE_BACKLIGHT_PWM_MIN_PERCENT
    int "Duty floor under a non-zero brightness (percent)"
    depends on SLATE_BACKLIGHT_PWM
    default 7
    range 1 50
    help
        The bottom of the range is dark rather than dim on this panel. A
        non-zero brightness is mapped into the band between this floor and full
        duty, so brightness 1 is the dimmest light the panel actually produces
        instead of an unlit screen.

        Which is why the range starts at 1 rather than 0. A floor of zero would
        make brightness 1 an unlit panel that still reports itself as lit, and
        the touch boundary reads that report to decide whether a press is a wake
        or a tile: black glass, and the light in the living room goes on.

        7 is the ESPHome package's min_power of 0.07. The thread that package
        cites recommends 0.3 for the same board, which is a wide enough
        disagreement to say that neither number has been measured here: expect
        to re-measure it on yours.

        Zero brightness is still zero duty, and the enable output goes with it.

endmenu
